<h2>Damage That Is Easy to Miss</h2>
<p>The burned area is obvious. These items are often underestimated or left out of the first estimate:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Smoke and soot</strong> in rooms the fire never reached, inside cabinets, closets, and wall cavities. See <a href="/services/smoke-damage-claims/">smoke damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC contamination.</strong> Ductwork, air handlers, and returns can spread soot and odor through the whole building.</li>
<li><strong>Water from firefighting.</strong> Wet drywall, insulation, subfloor, and flooring need drying or replacement, and mold can follow if drying is delayed. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Heat damage.</strong> Warped windows, melted wiring insulation, cracked tile, and damaged roof decking near, but not in, the burn area.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical systems.</strong> Wiring exposed to heat or water may need inspection and replacement, and CPS Energy service may need to be restored after inspection.</li>
<li><strong>Odor.</strong> Smoke odor can remain in framing and porous materials after surface cleaning, which affects whether materials should be cleaned or replaced.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Local Construction and Rebuilding Issues</h2>
<p>San Antonio's housing stock affects fire claims. Older pier-and-beam homes in historic districts such as King William, Monte Vista, and Tobin Hill can have original wood framing, plaster walls, and features that are expensive to match, and exterior changes in designated historic districts may need city historic review in addition to a building permit. Newer slab-on-grade homes with stucco, limestone, or brick veneer may have masonry that survived the fire while the framing behind it did not. Repairs inside city limits generally require permits through the City of San Antonio Development Services Department and must meet the city's adopted International Residential Code and International Building Code. Suburban cities such as <a href="/service-areas/alamo-heights/">Alamo Heights</a> and Schertz have their own permit offices. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</p>

