<h2>Common Fire Claim Disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clean vs. replace.</strong> Insurers often propose cleaning smoke-affected materials and contents; owners may find that odor and residue remain.</li>
<li><strong>Scope of repairs.</strong> The estimate may cover only the burned room, not adjacent areas affected by heat, smoke, or water.</li>
<li><strong>Contents depreciation.</strong> Personal property is often paid at actual cash value first, with replacement cost released after items are replaced, if the policy provides it. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Living expense duration.</strong> Disagreements about how long repairs should reasonably take.</li>
<li><strong>Underinsurance.</strong> When the dwelling limit is lower than the actual rebuilding cost, the policy limit caps what can be paid.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Lightning, Wildfire Smoke, and Other Causes</h2>
<p>Not every fire claim starts in the kitchen. Lightning strikes during spring thunderstorms can start attic fires or damage electrical systems and equipment without visible flames. Fires that start at a neighboring property can damage your home through radiant heat and smoke. Each of these is usually handled under the same fire, lightning, or smoke perils of your policy, but documenting the cause and the date is important, especially when a lightning strike damaged electronics or HVAC equipment rather than the structure.</p>

<h2>Proof of Loss and Insurer Timelines</h2>
<p>Many policies require a sworn proof of loss within a stated time after the insurer asks for one. Under the Texas prompt-payment rules in Insurance Code Chapter 542, an insurer generally must acknowledge a claim and request the information it needs within 15 days, then accept or reject the claim within 15 business days after receiving everything it requested, unless it explains why it needs more time. A large fire claim may be paid in stages: an advance for living expenses, then building and contents payments as each is agreed. If your claim has stalled, see <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>How San Antonio Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurer. On a fire loss, we may document the structure room by room (including Matterport 3D imaging where useful), prepare a line-item building estimate in Xactimate, help organize the contents inventory, track additional living expenses, and communicate with the insurer's adjuster so you are not managing every call yourself. If the insurer's estimate leaves out smoke, water, or code items, we can respond with documentation, and if the dispute is over the amount of loss, we can explain how <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may apply.</p>
<p>We cannot guarantee a result; coverage and payment depend on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Texas also gives you 72 hours to rescind a public adjuster contract after signing.</p>

<h2>First Steps After a Fire</h2>
<ol>
<li>Make sure everyone is safe and do not enter until the fire department says it is safe.</li>
<li>Report the claim and ask the insurer about emergency living expenses.</li>
<li>Get a copy of the fire department incident report.</li>
<li>Secure the property: board-up, tarping, and fencing if needed. Keep receipts.</li>
<li>Photograph and video every room before cleanup or demolition.</li>
<li>Start a contents list and save receipts for everything you have to buy.</li>
</ol>
